  <info><![CDATA[# WHAT IS IT?

This is a model simulating sex and sexual orientation differences in short-term mating behaviors. Findings in evolutionary psychology suggest that males have a stronger desire for short-term mating than females (Regan & Atkins, 2006). They also have lower standards for a short-term mating partner (Buss & Schmitt, 1993). This model explores how males and females (heterosexual or homosexual) may behave differently in short-term mating under these assumptions. A systematic report and discussion of the experiments conducted using this model can be found in the paper "An Agent-Based Model of Sex and Sexual Orientation Differences in Short-Term Mating Behaviors as a Result of Mating Preferences" (Ying et al., 2024).

&nbsp;  

# HOW IT WORKS

The model runs with 300 agents in total. Agents in this model behave under the following rules on each tick:

* Move to a random point near where they were born and decide on whether they would like a short-term mating (i.e., whether they have a preference on short-term mating) on this tick

* If they meet someone else, the two agents have a short-term mating if (a) they both have a preference for short-term mating on this tick, (b) they both meet each other’s standards for a short-term mating partner, (c) the pair is of different sexes in the all-heterosexual model or is of the same sex in the all-homosexual model

*  If an agent did not have a preference for short-term mating, or had such a preference but did not actually have short-term mating on this tick, they may decide to form a long-term relationship with another agent they meet. This happens by a probability specified by an adjustable parameter (see below). The model visually shows the long-term relationship status by fixing the positions of the agents and turning their patch color grey. The coupled agents reduce their likelihood of choosing short-term mating.

&nbsp;  

# HOW TO USE IT
## Setup and Run the Model

Press **setup** to initialize the model, then press **go forever** to start the simulation. 

Note: Changes in the following sliders and switches do not take effect until the **setup** button is pressed.

*  The **number-of-males** slider sets the number of males in the population.

* The **movement-range** slider controls how far agents can go away from their birth (spawning) point. Setting movement-range to 15 allows agents to reach
nearly every patch in the view, and gives all agents a chance to meet.

* The **long-term-likelihood** slider sets the likelihood of forming a long-term relationship when two agents meet.

* The **male-willingness** and **female-willingness** sliders set the likelihood that a male/female agent chooses to have a short-term mating on each tick.

* The **male-desirability-standard** and **female-desirability-standard** sliders set male/female agents’ standard for a short-term mating partner. (Note: All agents’ desirability values are drawn from a normal distribution with a mean of 5 and a standard deviation of 1.5. This means that around 68% of all agents have a desirability value lying within 3.5-6.5, and 95% have a value lying within 2-8.)

* The **all-homosexual?** switch, when set on, turns everyone into gay males and lesbian females. Agents will only have short-term matings and/or form a long-term relationship with someone of the same sex.

## Observe the Model

There are six plots by which you can observe the model:

* The **Coupled Agents** plot shows the percentage of males and females in long-term relationships.

* The **In-Pool Agents** plot shows the percentage of males and females who have had at least one short-term mating encounter.

* The **Encounters (All)** plot shows the mean number of short-term matings encounters experienced by males and females in the entire population.

* The **Encounters (In-Pool)** plot shows the mean number of short-term matings encounters experienced only by those males and females who have had at least one short-term mating encounter.

* The **Unique Partners (All)** plot shows the mean number of short-term mating partners of males and females in the entire population.

* The **Unique Partners (In-Pool)** plot shows the mean number of short-term mating partners of only those males and females who have had at least one short-term mating encounter.


## Things to Notice

The default model parameter settings assume that a) the sex ratio is balanced in the population, b) everyone is heterosexual, c) males have a higher willingness to engage in short-term mating than females, and d) females have a higher desirability standard for short-term mating partners than males. Under these assumptions, several interesting things are worth noticing:

* The average number of short-term mating experiences and unique short-term partners is exactly the same in the full population of males and females, as shown by the ostensible single curves in the left-hand plots (in reality, the trace for females perfectly overlaps the trace for males).

* These numbers differ, however, between the population of males and females who are in the short-term mating pool (right-hand plots): males have visibly more short-term mating experiences and partners than females on average.

* There are more females than males in the short-term mating pool. Why is this the case and how can this explain the above observations?


## Things to Try

After you play with the default parameter setting, you can explore the model by trying the following things:

* Adjust the **male-willingness** and **female-willingness** sliders. What happens when males have the same willingness to engage in short-term mating as females? What about when females have higher willingness than males?

* Adjust the **male-desirability-standard** and **female-desirability-standard** sliders. What happens when males have the same standard as females? What about when females have higher standards than males?

*  Adjust the **number-of-males** slider to simulate an unbalanced sex ratio. How do the results change and what might be an explanation? You may want to look at both the cases where males outnumber females and the reverse.

* Set the **all-homosexual?** switch to true. To start with, you may set all other parameters to the default settings. How do the results change and what might be an explanation?

&nbsp;  

# CREDITS AND REFERENCES

Buss, D. M., & Schmitt, D. P. (1993). Sexual strategies theory: An evolutionary perspective on human mating. *Psychological Review, 100*(2), 204–232. https://doi.org/doi:10.1037/0033-295X.100.2.204d

Regan, P. C., & Atkins, L. (2006). Sex differences and similarities in frequency and intensity of sexual desire. *Social Behavior and Personality: An International Journal, 34*(1), 95–102. https://doi.org/10.2224/sbp.2006.34.1.95

Ying, Y., Antfolk, J., & Santtila, P. (2024). An Agent-Based Model of Sex and Sexual Orientation Differences in Short-Term Mating Behaviors as a Result of Mating Preferences. The Journal of Sex Research, 61(6), 946–954. https://doi.org/10.1080/00224499.2023.2183485

Ying, Y., Antfolk, J., &amp; Santtila, P. (in press). Mating preferences and short-term mating behavior: A simple agent-based model of sex and sexual-orientation differences (Ch. 13). In C. A. Coen &amp; J. R. Larson, Jr. (Eds.). Agent-based modeling for research on groups, networks, and organization. American Psychological Association.

&nbsp;]]></info>
